## Migration Analysis

Your code has **3 patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch` block with rollback | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Prevents rethrow to continue loop | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (moved from ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just let the loop condition exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals the token — replaces Thread.Abort

        // Give the thread time to finish cooperatively
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in .NET 8.
            // If this happens, GetNextItem/ProcessItem must be fixed
            // to check the token more frequently.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`_running` → `CancellationToken`** — The volatile bool is replaced by a `CancellationTokenSource`. `Cancel()` replaces `Abort()`.

2. **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — The `when` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not someone else's.

3. **`ResetAbort()` removed** — No longer needed. After catching `OperationCanceledException`, the loop naturally re-checks `token.IsCancellationRequested` and exits.

4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(2s)`** — `Join` actually waits for the thread to finish instead of blindly sleeping.

5. **`IDisposable` added** — `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed to avoid timer/callback leaks.

## Critical Pitfall: Blocking Calls

Your comment says `GetNextItem()` may block. **This is the hardest part.** If it blocks indefinitely (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take()`), cancellation won't take effect until it unblocks. You **must** pass the token into blocking calls:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks forever, relies on Abort to interrupt
var item = _queue.Take();

// After (.NET 8) — wakes up when token is cancelled
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
```

If `GetNextItem` wraps a synchronous I/O call that has no `CancellationToken` overload, switch to its async equivalent or use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout)` polling.

## Other Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Not passing `token` through the full call chain | If `ProcessItem` calls a slow sub-method that ignores the token, cancellation stalls there |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` and swallowing it | Let it propagate everywhere except this top-level loop |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or token-aware API call |
